<p><strong>The Altnow-Smith Home</strong></p>
<p><strong>506 3rd St NW</strong></p>
<p>This house was built in 1912, by {% person "hugh-altnow" %}Dr. Hugh Altnow{% endperson %}, who lived in the home from 1912 to 1924. The mail-order home came pre-cut and was shipped to {% place "bingenheimer-mercantile-company" %}Bingenheimer Mercantile Company{% endplace %}.</p>
<p>The home is a classic Arts-and-Crafts bungalow design, a one-and-a-half story dwelling. It features built-in china cabinets and bookcases, box-beam ceilings, and original light fixtures. A particular feature of this home is that all the hardware matches, from the drawer pulls to the doorknobs. The foundation is very unique; it is made of prairie stone. The wide eaves of the home are typical bungalow design.</p>
<p>In the front yard of the home, the original hitching posts from the early {% place "saint-joseph-church" %}Saint Joseph Church{% endplace %} are displayed.</p>
<p>In 1924, Dr. Altnow, original owner of the home, decided to go on to further study of children’s diseases and sold it to {% person "cecil-smith" %}Dr. Cecil Smith{% endperson %}. Later, Dr. Altnow started the {% place "hennepin-county-clinic" %}Hennepin County Clinic{% endplace %} in Minneapolis, MN.</p>
<p>For 25 years Doctor Smith served as local physician for the {% place "northern-pacific-railroad" %}Northern Pacific Railroad{% endplace %}, as well as school physician for the {% place "youth-correctional-center" %}State Industrial School{% endplace %} (now known as the Youth Correctional Center). Dr. Smith was also founder of the {% place "custer-district-health-unit" %}Custer Health District{% endplace %}. The home was occupied by the Smith family from 1924 until the death of Dr. Smith’s wife, {% person "winifred-smith" %}Winifred{% endperson %}, in 1976. The home was then purchased by {% person "bob-feickert" %}Bob{% endperson %} and {% person "gloria-feickert" %}Gloria Feickert{% endperson %}, who lived in it until 1988. Bob and Gloria were employees of the {% place "bismarck-tribune" %}Bismarck Tribune{% endplace %}. Gloria had also worked for Dr. Smith when she was in high school.</p>
